1. Introduction

The AR HUD could enhance driving-related information (such as speed, warning signals, navigation arrows) to the windshield of the vehicle to maximize the use of visual resources and allow the driver to focus on safe driving (See Fig.1). It represents the most innovative development direction of intelligent vehicles and becomes an important HMI in the era of the Internet of Things. With the wide application of intelligent driving assistance system, the demand of AR HUD is rising rapidly, and its market space has a fast expansion. As the main connection points between the driver and the vehicle, these in-vehicle HMI demand great care and attention from manufacturers, designers and human factor engineers [1].

Fig. 1. AR HUD interface.
The AR HUD interface is an essential way to exchange information between drivers and automatic systems. It is also the terminal for information collection and output in intelligent vehicles. Consequently, the drivers execute and complete a different kind of tasks, such as perceive surrounding situation, navigation guidance [2], road recognition [3], and decision-making analysis [4] based on the real time graphical and text information displayed in AR HUD interface. But unlike the traditional displays, the virtual contents on the AR HUD are superimposed displayed optically superimposed on the real word though special optical system, which causes it to be transparent. As a result, the changeable environmental context and complex ambient light weaken the legibility of AR contents. And it also reduced the visual comfort and cognitive performance of drivers [5].
Based on the design principles of human-vehicle interaction system, AR HUD interface needs to meet drivers' recognition and reading of visual information efficiently, and reduce drivers' distraction and cognitive load during driving as well [6-7]. The AR system which poorly designed or ignored the principle of human factors is likely to fail to achieve its goal of helping human beings, and may even seriously damage human performance. As an ergonomics factor, content color directly affects driver performance, including visual sensitivity and fatigue. On the one hand, the dark color, such as violet, can achieves high luminance contrast, which means driver’s information sensitivity improved, but just perform well in a short time. On the other hand, the light color, such as yellow, has low luminance contrast for driver while gazing at AR HUD. They achieve better performance with low visual fatigue during a long time, but less sensitivity. Therefore, this paper is devoted to find the content color value that can comprehensively consider the driver’s visual sensitivity and fatigue, and quantify the selection process. This study also has reference significance for balancing situation awareness and cognitive load of drivers.
The paper is organized as follows. First, the main theories and methods which supporting the interface optimization model are introduced in Section 2. Then, the content color and its extended matter-elements of AR HUD interface are modeled and analysis in the Section 3. Section 4 describes two mathematical color selection model under two condition. An simple engineering application is proposed to show its application. Finally, section 5 summarizes the main conclusions and future works.

