There are many interface factors that affect the visual comfort and performance of driver, such as color combination, complexity of graphic, information density, layout and display contrast, etc. Individual or conjoint analysis can be conducted among those factors by one-dimensional or multi-dimensional matter-element. Since color combination is the most significant characteristic that affects visual comfort of the AR HUD interface [13], we analyze the color combination independently in this paper.
The virtual contents presented on the AR HUD are superimposed on the physical environment through complex optical systems, making it being transparent. Therefore, ambient light, AR content color and background color will also influence the color combination of the AR HUD interface perceived by drivers. The variation of ambient light mean that accurate color and consistently high contrast ratio should be maintained to adapt to the different driving conditions in any AR HUD solutions [14].
In this section, we will conduct modeling and extended analysis on the following matter elements from the perspective of man-machine-environment: content color (machine), brightness (machine), number of applicable environments(environment) and driver performance over time (man).

