3.3. Number of applicable environments

The display effect of the AR HUD projection perceived by human eyes is determined by the brightness of projected light and ambient light [16]. In AR HUD, the virtual image is viewed through transparent windshield, and the luminance contrast between the image and the background is a very critical factor which can affect visual performance and subjective preference [17]. Many articles have proposed that visual search performance improves with the increase of luminance contrast [18-20]. H.C. Weston's results show that the effect of luminance contrast in visual performance has always been critical, and no matter how high the brightness, poor contrast performance can never be compared with good contrast performance [21]. Ya-Hsien Ko also found that legibility was significantly impacted by luminance contrast, with low contrast sensitivity making it difficult to perceive faces, objects, or road signs [22].
Previous researches suggested that the visual display terminals should have a luminance contrast of at least 1:3 [2325]. For example, we suppose the brightness units are ranged from 0 to 100. When the content color has a brightness unit of 30, the environment should have a brightness unit within 0-10 and 90-100 (3 times unit difference) to ensure the readability in ambient light. We define the number of applicable environments as the number of ambient brightness unit that meet the display contrast requirements for a given content color brightness. Thus, the number of applicable environments is 22. In the same way, if the brightness unit of content color is 90, the number of applicable environments is 31(0-30).
Within a certain range, the AR content with high brightness can be applied to more environments, which means that drivers are more sensitive to the information presented by the AR HUD interface and have better driving performance in a short time. On the contrary, AR content with low brightness can be applied to fewer environments, resulting in lower driver sensitivity and poor driving performance in a short time. Therefore, if only the number of applicable environments is considered, content color with high brightness can achieve better driving performance in a short while than those with low brightness.
Then, the number of applicable environments is expanded to quantify the brightness, and its matter-element model is constructed as M3 and M3′ .

