3.4. Driver performance over time

However, there are also many studies have suggested that working with high screen brightness for long periods of time may cause visual fatigue. Simone et al. studied the interaction effect of screen brightness and ambient illuminance on visual fatigue and arousal during prolonged digital reading (1 hour). The results showed that both visual fatigue and arousal increased simultaneously under high screen brightness [26]. While the rise of arousal has a good connotation for improving reading performance, an upsurge in visual fatigue might be a bad new for our eyes. Wang et al. found that there was a negative correlation between the roadside landscape color and drivers' EEG δ wave components, that is, the higher the brightness of the landscape color, the more effective it was to avoid visual fatigue [27]. Joseph L. Gabbard and his colleagues indicated that repeatedly performing context switching and focal distance switching between a real and AR environment can cause the visual fatigue steadily increase and significantly reduce performance [28].
According to the literature review above, we conducted a qualitative analysis on brightness and visual fatigue degree. That is, the greater the luminance contrast, the stronger the stimulation to human eyes, prolonged viewing exacerbates visual fatigue. While the lower luminance contrast could reduce the sensitivity of the eye to flicker, leading to better fatigue resistance. Thus, if only driver performance over time (DOT) is considered, content colors with low brightness achieve better over long periods of time than the high one.

