4.4. Engineering application
Considering that different luminance contrasts have positive and negative effects on driver performance, the application of this model in design engineering is discussed in this section. For the content with low information priority and long viewing time, anti-fatigue should be considered first, which is reflected in the model as increasing the weight of DOT and reflected in the color as low brightness. If choose w% ≈ 20%, BL ≈ 65, then K(n) ≈ 2, K(t) ≈ 0, K′(x) = 20% ∗ K(n) + 80% ∗ K(t) = 0.4.
On the contrary, for the content with high information priority and short viewing time, reaction speed should be considered first, which is reflected in the model as increasing the weight of NAE, and reflected in the color as high brightness. If choose w% ≈ 100%, BL ≈ 100, then K(n) ≈ 0, K(t) ≈ 4, K′(x) = 1 ∗ K(n) + 0 ∗ K(t) = 0.
According to this model, optimization suggestions are proposed for the design application of AR HUD in the intelligent cockpit. The AR HUD interface content mainly consists of basic driving information, safety information and multimedia entertainment information. Basic driving information (including average speed, remaining fuel, map navigation, etc.) will be displayed continuously during driving, and a brightness of 65% is selected as the content color (w% ≈ 20%). Multimedia entertainment information (including weather, music, video, communication, etc.) is only viewed during operation, so this type of information has a short viewing time and low priority, and a brightness of 75% is selected as the content color (w% ≈ 30%). As for the safety information (including obstacle information, lane track information and traffic sign information), 100% brightness (w% ≈ 0%) is selected as the content color because

